---
import Navigation from "../../components/navigation.astro";
import { posts } from "../../data/posts.js";

const post = posts.find((post) => post.slug === "post_sixteen");

if (!post) {
  throw new Error("Post not found: post_sixteen");
}

const { title, date, dateTime, tags } = post;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>{title} | Ivy Duncan</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Why I called my romance books the Proximity Series, how the trope connects the books, and the other series I have planned."
    />
  </head>

  <body>
    <Navigation />

    <main class="container">
      <article class="blog-post">
        <header class="post-header">
          <a href="/blog" class="back-link">&larr; Back to the blog</a>

          <h1>{title}</h1>

          <div class="post-meta">
            <time datetime={dateTime}>{date}</time>

            {tags.length > 0 && (
              <div class="post-tags">
                {tags.map((tag) => <span class="tag">{tag}</span>)}
              </div>
            )}
          </div>
        </header>

        <div class="post-content">
          <h2>Why Is the Series Called the Proximity Series?</h2>

          <p>
            This is kind of stupid, but I really hate coming up with book
            names. And I hate coming up with series names even more.
          </p>

          <p>
            The name <em>Proximity</em> came from the fact that all the books
            in the series have a proximity trope in some way.
          </p>

          <p>Here's how it works for each book:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Books One and Two:</strong> The characters are
              neighbors who are forced into each other's lives because
              their respective roommates are dating each other.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Book Three:</strong> The characters are in the same
              class and sit right next to each other. They also frequent the
              same places on campus, including the library and coffee shop.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Book Four:</strong> <em>(Spoilers!)</em> Roommates.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Now, this is kind of a stupid reason to name a series, because
            when you think about it, most romance books have some kind of
            proximity trope in them.
          </p>

          <p>
            For example, <em>Five Years and 2,000 Miles</em> has Sloan and
            Leo in a class together. So, technically, that has proximity,
            too.
          </p>

          <p>
            But I don't know what else I would have called the series, so
            <em>Proximity</em> it is!
          </p>

          <h2>What's Next?</h2>

          <p>
            I have two more series planned for after the Proximity Series
            concludes.
          </p>

          <p>
            One I'm not ready to talk about publicly yet, but that one
            already has a series name picked out. So at least I managed to
            make that decision ahead of time!
          </p>

          <p>
            The other has a reconnection theme. I have three stories planned
            about couples who knew each other when they were younger and
            then reconnected as adults.
          </p>

          <p>
            I haven't figured out what I'm going to call that series yet.
            Apparently, coming up with series names is going to remain a
            problem for me.
          </p>
        </div>

        <footer class="post-footer">
          <a href="/blog" class="back-link">&larr; Back to all posts</a>
        </footer>
      </article>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  :global(body) {
    margin: 0;
    background: #fffaf4;
    color: #3f3a35;
    font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .container {
    width: min(800px, calc(100% - 3rem));
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 3rem 0 5rem;
  }

  .blog-post {
    width: 100%;
  }

  .post-header {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
    padding-bottom: 1.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd6cd;
  }

  .back-link {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    color: #765f48;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem);
    line-height: 1.2;
    font-weight: normal;
  }

  .post-meta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    color: #6f6a63;
    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .post-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  .tag {
    padding: 0.2rem 0.65rem;
    border: 1px solid #ddd6cd;
    border-radius: 20px;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
  }

  .post-content h2 {
    margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem;
    color: #765f48;
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    line-height: 1.35;
    font-weight: normal;
  }

  .post-content p {
    margin: 0 0 1.4rem;
  }

  .post-content ul {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
  }

  .post-content li {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    padding-left: 0.25rem;
  }

  .post-content strong {
    color: #3f3a35;
  }

  .post-footer {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid #ddd6cd;
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .container {
      width: calc(100% - 2rem);
      padding-top: 2rem;
    }

    .post-header {
      margin-bottom: 2rem;
    }

    .post-content h2 {
      font-size: 1.4rem;
    }
  }
</style>